---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ContactList from "../components/ContactList.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
    title="About — Software Security"
    description="Andrew Lilley Brinker is a Member of Technical Staff at Anthropic, writing about software security, memory safety, Rust, and open source."
    socialImage="/social/about.png"
    socialImageAlt="About — Software Security"
>
    <h1>About</h1>
    <div
        class="about-photo-frame inline-block relative group max-w-36 md:max-w-48 transition-transform duration-300 rotate-3 hover:rotate-0 float-right ml-4 md:ml-8 mb-4 md:mb-8 -top-10"
    >
        <img
            src="/image/andrew.png"
            alt="Grayscale and dithered picture of Andrew"
            width="500"
            height="500"
            loading="eager"
            class="!m-0 rounded-md block"
        />
    </div>
    <p>
        I’m a Member of Technical Staff at <a href="https://www.anthropic.com"
            >Anthropic</a
        >.
    </p>
    <p>
        I want the people who build and maintain software to be able to make
        informed decisions about what they depend on. Before Anthropic, I worked
        on software security, vulnerability management, and open source at <a
            href="https://www.mitre.org">MITRE</a
        >. There I created <a href="https://github.com/mitre/hipcheck"
            >Hipcheck</a
        >, a tool that helps maintainers and users weigh the supply-chain risk
        of their dependencies, and led its redesign around plugins.
    </p>
    <p>
        I’ve also worked on the standards and policy that shape how software is
        identified and tracked. On the <a href="https://www.cve.org/">CVE</a> system,
        I improved authentication and authorization, added support for <a
            href="https://packageurl.org/">Package URLs</a
        > in CVE Records, and led the Quality Working Group’s adoption of a Request
        for Discussion process for considering proposals. On the <a
            href="https://omnibor.io/">OmniBOR</a
        > Core Team, I led the creation of its <a
            href="https://github.com/omnibor/omnibor-rs"
            >Rust library and the omnibor CLI</a
        > and worked on universally reproducible, fine-grained identifiers for
        software artifacts. I’ve advised federal agencies, including the
        Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency and the Department of
        Defense, on software policy such as SBOMs and open source security.
    </p>
    <p>
        Memory safety matters to me because it makes software safer by default.
        In <a href="https://queue.acm.org/detail.cfm?id=3773095"
            >“Memory Safety for Skeptics”</a
        >, published in <em>ACM Queue</em> and <em>Communications of the ACM</em>,
        I make the case for pursuing it amid competing priorities.
    </p>
    <p>
        I’ve taught Rust at MITRE and programming language theory at <a
            href="https://cse.csusb.edu">CSUSB</a
        >, spoken at <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0PhfaFkzdBA"
            >RustConf</a
        > and <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Wz2oFEDwiOk"
            >Rust Belt Rust</a
        >, contributed to the official Rust documentation and the Rustonomicon,
        and edited <a
            href="https://www.manning.com/books/write-powerful-rust-macros"
            >a book on Rust procedural macros</a
        >.
    </p>
    <p class="not-prose mt-8">
        <a
            href="/resume.pdf"
            class="inline-flex h-8 shrink-0 items-center gap-2 rounded-full bg-neutral-500/10 pl-3.5 pr-2 font-sans text-sm text-neutral-600 hover:bg-neutral-500/15 hover:text-black dark:text-muted dark:hover:text-white"
            >Résumé <span class="tag">PDF</span></a
        >
    </p>
    <aside class="name-plate" aria-labelledby="my-name">
        <p id="my-name" class="name-plate-label not-prose">My Name</p>
        <p class="name-plate-name not-prose">
            <span class="name-plate-part"><span class="name-plate-word">Andrew</span><span class="name-plate-bracket">first name</span></span>
            <span class="name-plate-part"><span class="name-plate-word">Lilley Brinker</span><span class="name-plate-bracket">last name</span></span>
        </p>
        <p>
            “Lilley Brinker” is my full last name. Two parts, no hyphen. When my
            wife and I got married we combined our last names without a hyphen.
            My last name is not “Brinker” and it is not correct to call me “Mr.
            Brinker”.
        </p>
        <p>“Lilley” is pronounced like the word “lily”.</p>
    </aside>
    <h2 id="where-to-find-me" class="not-prose mt-16 mb-6 font-sans text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-muted">Social / Contact</h2>
    <ContactList />
</BaseLayout>

<script is:inline>
    // Talks and the AI policy were once sections of this page; old links to
    // them go to their own pages now.
    {
        const moved = { "#talks": "/talks/", "#ai-policy": "/ai/" }[location.hash];
        if (moved) location.replace(moved);
    }
</script>
